.he "net (4)                     10/21/26                       net (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
net
- the network card's frame channel
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
The network card is a character device, major 5 minor 0, one in a
machine.  It is a byte channel to an ESP32 that owns the network: the
card does tcp/ip and wifi, the guest moves framed commands and data
through it.  The contract - framing, socket states and error codes - is
sys/NET.md; this page is the device's use of it.
.sp
The guest sees two S-100 ports.  NETDATA, 0x60, reads a byte the card
has pushed and writes a byte for it to assemble; NETSTAT, 0x61, holds
RXRDY (an incoming byte waits), TXRDY (a byte can be written) and RDY
(the card has a network: JOIN has finished).  The card raises a vectored
interrupt line while RXRDY is set and drops it when the byte is read.
.sp
write() pushes a frame: a four byte header - type, socket, length low,
length high - then the payload.  read() returns one pushed frame in the
same shape, header first; a frame is consumed whole even when the
caller's buffer is short.  There is no seek, no raw command block and no
geometry; formatting, ioctl and geometry mean nothing here and are
refused.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FRAMES
.in +5
.br
The guest's commands are OPEN, CLOSE, BIND, CONNECT, LISTEN, SEND,
STATUS and JOIN, and the card answers with ACK, pushes received bytes as
DATA and reports asynchronous changes as EVENT.  JOIN puts the card on
its network and answers with the address DHCP gave it.  Eight sockets,
one connection each; the socket is in the frame header, so one device
carries all eight.  The faces built on it are tftp, the network disk
(see nd in section 4) and rlogin; rcp in section 1 speaks rsh over a
tcp socket.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PROVISIONING
.in +5
.br
The card is given its wifi network three ways, and all three store the
ssid and password in the card's non-volatile memory: its own setup
access point, a USB-serial editor, and this interface.  GETCONF returns
the stored pair and SETCONF stores and applies one at once, as frames on
socket 0xff; netconf in section 1 drives them.  Everything after the
pair - address, mask, gateway, DNS - is DHCP.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
netconf(1), rcp(1), nd(4), sys/NET.md
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
The driver polls RXRDY and busy-waits for a frame; a caller that reads
when no frame is coming stops the machine.  Read only when one is
expected until the interrupt handler lands.
